
\begin{columns}[t]

\separatorcolumn

\begin{column}{0.3\paperwidth}

\begin{block}{Motivation}
    Rust's crate ecosystem (\texttt{crates.io}) contains over \textbf{264,000 crates} with billions of downloads, however it is understudied compared to other ecosystems like npm and PyPI.
    Supply chain attacks are practical and effective, and become more popular year over year, a single compromised crate can propagate code to millions of downstream projects.

    \bigskip
    \textbf{Key risks in the Rust supply chain:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \texttt{build.rs} scripts allows arbitrary code execution at compile time (network, process spawning, etc.)
        \item Typosquatting and malicious crate uploads already observed in the wild in other ecosystems
        \item No existing framework integrates vulnerability scanning, secret detection, build script heuristics, typosquatting, and LLM-based analysis into a single pipeline
    \end{itemize}
\end{block}

\begin{block}{Objectives \& Scope}
    \heading{Core Objective}
    Design, implement and evaluate a scalable security analysis platform that unifies six analysis modules and processes the entire crates.io ecosystem.

    \bigskip
    \heading{Scope}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Over 264,000 crates from the official crates.io database dump
        \item Source code analysis via shallow git clones of the latest commit
        \item 6 integrated analysis modules in parallel execution
        \item Redis-based distributed work queue with topological ordering
        \item Optimized PostgreSQL database
        \item Web dashboard
        \item Containerized deployment (Docker Compose, scalable worker count)
    \end{itemize}
\end{block}

\begin{exampleblock}{System Architecture}
    \heading{Data Pipeline}
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Parse \textbf{crates.io} CSV dump to extract crate metadata \& dependency graph
        \item Populate Redis queue with \textbf{leaf crates} (no dependencies first)
        \item Workers claim crates via \texttt{BRPOPLPUSH}, clone source, run the analysis modules in parallel
        \item Mark crate done, promote dependents via Lua scripts
        \item Periodic cargo cache cleaning, results stored in PostgreSQL
    \end{enumerate}

    \bigskip
    \heading{Technology Stack}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Core}: Rust (tokio, clap CLI, diesel ORM)
        \item \textbf{Queue}: Redis (lists, sets, Lua scripting for atomic operations)
        \item \textbf{DB}: PostgreSQL (7 tables, indexes)
        \item \textbf{Deploy}: Docker Compose, tmpfs ramdisk, multi-stage Dockerfiles
        \item \textbf{Web}: Axum + Tower-HTTP REST API, backend rendering, vanilla JS dashboard with Chart.js
    \end{itemize}
\end{exampleblock}

\end{column}

\separatorcolumn

\begin{column}{0.3\paperwidth}

\begin{block}{Six Integrated Analysis Modules}
    \heading{1.\ \textcolor{UVTDarkBlue}{Cargo audit Vulnerability Scanning}}
    Cross-references the \texttt{Cargo.lock} file against the RustSec Advisory Database.
    Computes CVSS 3.1 scores. Detects unmaintained and yanked crates besides vulnerabilities.

    \bigskip
    \heading{2.\ \textcolor{UVTDarkBlue}{Gitleaks Secret Detection}}
    Scans source for 150+ secret patterns (API keys, tokens, private keys, credentials).
    Excludes test and example directories. Generates per-file findings with line numbers.

    \bigskip
    \heading{3.\ \textcolor{UVTDarkBlue}{Build.rs Heuristic Analysis}}
    Six rule-based checks on build scripts:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Network calls (URLs, IPs, sockets)
        \item Process spawning (\texttt{Command}, \texttt{std::process})
        \item Raw IP addresses and suspicious TLDs (\texttt{.tk}, \texttt{.xyz}, etc.)
        \item High-entropy strings (possible obfuscation)
    \end{itemize}

    \bigskip
    \heading{4.\ \textcolor{UVTDarkBlue}{LLM-Based Malicious Code Scoring}}
    Locally-hosted LM Studio model scores crate source for
    malicious intent. Treated as an optional module

    \bigskip
    \heading{5.\ \textcolor{UVTDarkBlue}{Typosquatting Detection}}
    Five string-similarity algorithms combined with weighted scoring:
    Levenshtein, Damerau-Levenshtein, Jaro-Winkler, keyboard proximity,
    prefix similarity. Compares every crate name against all others.

    \bigskip
    \heading{6.\ \textcolor{UVTDarkBlue}{Transitive Risk Propagation}}
    Dependency graph analysis that propagates vulnerability and risk scores
    \textbf{transitively} through the entire dependency tree.
\end{block}

\begin{block}{Performance Characteristics}
    \begin{table}
    \centering
    \caption{Per-crate analysis time (226,546 crates)}
    \begin{tabular}{@{} lr @{}}
        \toprule
        Metric & Value \\
        \midrule
        Median & \textbf{1.46 s} \\
        Mean & 15.1 s \\
        P95 & 86.8 s \\
        Max & 52.1 min \\
        \midrule
        Bottleneck & \texttt{cargo-audit} (74\% of mean time) \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    \bigskip
    Horizontal scaling with topological ordering ensures dependencies are always processed before their dependents.
    Cycle detection via DFS at seeding time.
\end{block}

\end{column}

\separatorcolumn

\begin{column}{0.3\paperwidth}

\begin{alertblock}{Key Findings}
    \heading{Dataset}
    226,546 crates scanned successfully (85.6\% of 264,750 total).\\
    13\% failed due to missing \texttt{Cargo.lock} or inaccessible repositories.

    \bigskip
    \heading{Vulnerabilities}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{32.1\%} of scanned crates have at least one known vulnerability (72,773 crates)
        \item Most frequent advisory: RUSTSEC-2023-0071 (12,487 occurrences)
        \item \textbf{82.6\%} informational severity; only 11\% High/Critical
        \item Crate age < 6 months: \textbf{30.9\% vuln rate} (higher than average)
        \item High-download-velocity crates: only \textbf{25.2\% vuln rate}
    \end{itemize}

    \bigskip
    \heading{Secrets \& Build Scripts}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 882,955 secrets found across 18,571 crates
        \item 95.8\% generic-api-key matches (almost all are false positives)
        \item 561 crates leak GitHub PATs
        \item 3,194 crates (1.41\%) spawn processes in \texttt{build.rs}
        \item 1,005 crates (0.44\%) make network calls in \texttt{build.rs}
    \end{itemize}
\end{alertblock}

\begin{alertblock}{Critical Insights}
    Crates shipping binaries have a \textbf{67.7\% vulnerability rate}, more than double the ecosystem average (32.1\%).
    \textbf{38,495} name-similarity pairs detected; \textbf{891 pairs} with
    scores $\geq$ 95. However this is mostly due to crates.io crate naming patterns.
    \heading{Substrate/Polkadot: \#1 Risk Source}
    The largest single source of transitive vulnerability risk. \texttt{sp-runtime}: 567 dependents, 41 vulnerabilities.
    The top 100 most-vulnerable crates contain a large fraction of all ecosystem vulnerabilities. Th dependency network counts over \textbf{1.7 million} relationships.
\end{alertblock}

\begin{block}{Conclusions \& Future Work}
    This thesis provides the \textbf{first large-scale, multi-modal security
    characterization of crates.io}. The framework is open-source and designed
    for potential Rust Foundation adoption, however with many required changes, and sufficiently powerful hardware.
    \heading{Future Directions}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Version-specific analysis (track vulnerabilities per release)
        \item Real-time monitoring of new crate publications
        \item Sandboxed \texttt{build.rs} execution for dynamic analysis
        \item Repository caching layer to reduce git clone overhead
        \item Storing everything possible in RAM, more caching
    \end{itemize}

